1125:A \emph{wheel} $W= (H,x)$ is a graph formed by a hole $H$ (called the \emph{rim}) together with a vertex $x$ (called the \emph{center}) that has at least three neighbors in the hole.
1126:Prism, pyramids, thetas, and wheels are referred to as \emph{Truemper configurations}; see \cref{f:tc}.
1128:A \emph{sector} of a wheel $(H,x)$ is a path $P$ that is contained in $H$, whose endpoints are neighbors of $x$ and whose internal vertices are not.
1129:Note that $H$ is edgewise partitioned into the sectors of $(H,x)$.
1130:Also, every wheel has at least three sectors.
1131:A wheel is \emph{broken} if at least two of its sectors have length at least~2.
